WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.150
- You know for two guys who
live in California you'd think

00:00:03.150 --> 00:00:05.700
we know how to dress for
the beach a little better.

00:00:07.010 --> 00:00:08.700
- I got this.

00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:09.533
- Fish!

00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:11.233
- In Hawaii.

00:00:11.233 --> 00:00:12.540
- Because if your gonna eat seafood

00:00:12.540 --> 00:00:14.570
might as well go to the
middle of the ocean to get it.

00:00:14.570 --> 00:00:15.403
- [Steven] Today on-

00:00:15.403 --> 00:00:16.236
- [Andrew] Worth It.

00:00:16.236 --> 00:00:17.070
- [Steven] We're going to be trying three

00:00:17.070 --> 00:00:19.010
fish dishes at three drastically
different price points

00:00:19.010 --> 00:00:20.440
to find out which fish dish

00:00:20.440 --> 00:00:21.870
is the most worth it at its price.

00:00:21.870 --> 00:00:23.000
- Fish dish bish.

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:23.883
Look at Hawaii.

00:00:24.870 --> 00:00:25.770
That's actually the ocean.

00:00:25.770 --> 00:00:26.640
That's Hawaii.

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:28.620
We are on the island of Oahu.

00:00:28.620 --> 00:00:30.140
- Oh.

00:00:30.140 --> 00:00:31.220
In the city of--

00:00:31.220 --> 00:00:32.110
- Honolulu.

00:00:32.110 --> 00:00:33.680
- This will be pretty
much gonna be sticking

00:00:33.680 --> 00:00:34.513
for this journey.

00:00:34.513 --> 00:00:36.170
Maybe we'll come back some other day.

00:00:36.170 --> 00:00:37.240
Check out the other islands.

00:00:37.240 --> 00:00:40.220
- First off we're going to a
place called Ahi Assassins.

00:00:40.220 --> 00:00:41.451
- What are we going to be having there?

00:00:41.451 --> 00:00:42.620
- Poke!

00:00:42.620 --> 00:00:44.480
- Wish I brought my swim trunks.

00:00:44.480 --> 00:00:46.204
I didn't pack for this really.

00:00:46.204 --> 00:00:48.130
(band music)

00:00:48.130 --> 00:00:50.590
- I'm Erika, I'm the
co-owner of Ahi Assassins.

00:00:50.590 --> 00:00:52.420
- I'm Josh, co-owner of Ahi Assassins.

00:00:52.420 --> 00:00:54.500
- This is traditional
poke here that we do.

00:00:54.500 --> 00:00:55.333
- What is poke?

00:00:55.333 --> 00:00:57.340
- [Erika] In Hawaiian
just means to dice or cut.

00:00:57.340 --> 00:00:59.840
Poke for us is fresh fish, lightly sauced.

00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:01.870
We wanna bring through
the taste of the fish.

00:01:01.870 --> 00:01:04.940
Majority of the time if you
come in here in our first hours

00:01:04.940 --> 00:01:06.810
you'll see guys breaking
down fish, right here.

00:01:06.810 --> 00:01:07.940
- [Andrew] How long have you been fishing?

00:01:07.940 --> 00:01:09.793
- I think I was made on a boat.

00:01:09.793 --> 00:01:10.626
(laughing)

00:01:10.626 --> 00:01:12.900
Possibly, born right off of a boat.

00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:13.733
On my mom's side

00:01:13.733 --> 00:01:15.711
I come from a long line
of Hawaiian fisherman.

00:01:15.711 --> 00:01:16.870
- So did you always think that you would

00:01:16.870 --> 00:01:18.620
be a fisherman for work?

00:01:18.620 --> 00:01:19.500
- It was my dream.

00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:20.660
I read the "Old Man in the Sea".

00:01:20.660 --> 00:01:22.370
I'm like, that's who I wanna be.

00:01:22.370 --> 00:01:23.920
- [Erika] He was a construction worker.

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:25.200
He would never go to work

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:27.340
because he wanted to catch the ahi.

00:01:27.340 --> 00:01:29.020
So we decided to give it a shot

00:01:29.020 --> 00:01:30.290
and make it a business.

00:01:30.290 --> 00:01:31.950
- [Josh] This is all that's
left of our first boat.

00:01:31.950 --> 00:01:34.129
- I was wondering, like it's
kind of a weird looking door.

00:01:34.129 --> 00:01:34.962
(laughing)

00:01:34.962 --> 00:01:36.430
- This boat now rests at
the bottom of the ocean.

00:01:36.430 --> 00:01:38.620
We've still got the door ahis
are still coming through it.

00:01:38.620 --> 00:01:40.110
- Do you have a couple favorites

00:01:40.110 --> 00:01:41.160
that you could recommend for us?

00:01:41.160 --> 00:01:44.250
- Yeah so we are really
popular for our Lunatic poke.

00:01:44.250 --> 00:01:45.860
That's our version of spicy ahi.

00:01:45.860 --> 00:01:47.800
I am not a mayonnaise eater

00:01:47.800 --> 00:01:50.020
so we decided to take the mayonnaise out

00:01:50.020 --> 00:01:51.880
and add a few other things in there.

00:01:51.880 --> 00:01:53.550
- Could you give us a little snapshot

00:01:53.550 --> 00:01:54.383
of what is in that sauce?

00:01:54.383 --> 00:01:56.369
- If I tell you, I gotta kill you man.

00:01:56.369 --> 00:01:58.400
(laughing)

00:01:58.400 --> 00:01:59.550
- [Andrew] Talking to
a couple of assassins

00:01:59.550 --> 00:02:01.920
I don't want to rub them the wrong way.

00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:05.030
- This is a miniature van that
we're sitting in the trunk of

00:02:05.030 --> 00:02:07.810
because Ahi Assassins does
not have any sit down space

00:02:07.810 --> 00:02:08.643
in their retail space.

00:02:08.643 --> 00:02:10.940
- You take a bowl and you eat it anywhere.

00:02:10.940 --> 00:02:13.150
- I feel like I'm channeling
Josh's story right now.

00:02:13.150 --> 00:02:14.740
I got sounds of construction.

00:02:14.740 --> 00:02:16.190
All I wanna do is eat fish.

00:02:16.190 --> 00:02:17.023
- [Steven] Let's do it.

00:02:17.023 --> 00:02:17.856
So first.

00:02:17.856 --> 00:02:18.689
- [Andrew] The Lunatic.

00:02:18.689 --> 00:02:19.522
- [Steven] Yes.

00:02:19.522 --> 00:02:22.020
- Looks like cartoon
rubies, little sea jewels.

00:02:22.020 --> 00:02:23.630
- [Steven] Look at how juicy they look.

00:02:23.630 --> 00:02:24.730
- It looks extremely juicy

00:02:24.730 --> 00:02:25.940
- I can't do much more talking

00:02:25.940 --> 00:02:27.580
because I'm salivating so much.

00:02:27.580 --> 00:02:29.114
- Cheers.

00:02:29.114 --> 00:02:30.265
(upbeat music)

00:02:30.265 --> 00:02:31.390
(chewing)

00:02:31.390 --> 00:02:32.223
Oh, my god.

00:02:32.223 --> 00:02:33.056
- Yeah.

00:02:33.056 --> 00:02:34.060
- It's so tender.

00:02:34.060 --> 00:02:35.140
It just like melts.

00:02:35.140 --> 00:02:36.420
- This is the real tuna melt.

00:02:36.420 --> 00:02:37.253
- Yes.

00:02:37.253 --> 00:02:38.330
- This is the most luxurious texture

00:02:38.330 --> 00:02:39.670
I've ever experienced with food.

00:02:39.670 --> 00:02:41.990
I feel like a bear eating this.

00:02:41.990 --> 00:02:43.480
It's like (growling).

00:02:43.480 --> 00:02:45.030
- I've never had real--

00:02:45.030 --> 00:02:45.870
- Oh my god.

00:02:45.870 --> 00:02:47.380
- It's like when you watch sports

00:02:47.380 --> 00:02:48.650
and your just watching
it from your own couch.

00:02:48.650 --> 00:02:50.610
Then when you go the
game and your court side

00:02:50.610 --> 00:02:53.020
you actually see and feel
the vibes of the place.

00:02:53.020 --> 00:02:55.223
- Courtside with the bad fish.

00:02:56.370 --> 00:02:57.487
- No hesitation.

00:02:57.487 --> 00:02:59.880
(laughing)

00:02:59.880 --> 00:03:00.910
- [Erika] We're gonna do Hawaiian style,

00:03:00.910 --> 00:03:02.410
which is our traditional poke.

00:03:02.410 --> 00:03:04.440
- [Josh] Hawaiian salt,
limu is the seaweed.

00:03:04.440 --> 00:03:06.230
- [Erika] Throw white onions and inamona,

00:03:06.230 --> 00:03:07.730
a little bit of Hawaiian chili peppers,

00:03:07.730 --> 00:03:09.520
and then coat that with sesame oil.

00:03:09.520 --> 00:03:10.750
Mix everything around,

00:03:10.750 --> 00:03:11.990
get the rice in your bowl,

00:03:11.990 --> 00:03:14.118
top it on top and then
that's your final poke bowl.

00:03:14.118 --> 00:03:14.951
(cash register ring)

00:03:14.951 --> 00:03:16.860
- So here we have the Hawaiian style.

00:03:16.860 --> 00:03:18.123
- Yes.

00:03:18.123 --> 00:03:21.180
(band music)

00:03:21.180 --> 00:03:22.013
- Oh...

00:03:23.117 --> 00:03:25.100
God, what have you done?

00:03:25.100 --> 00:03:28.350
- This tastes like your
standing on a beach and just

00:03:28.350 --> 00:03:30.070
grab the tuna out of the water.

00:03:30.070 --> 00:03:32.080
You got all this seaweed stuck to it.

00:03:32.080 --> 00:03:32.913
- [Steven] Oh my god.

00:03:32.913 --> 00:03:33.746
- I want to try all of them.

00:03:33.746 --> 00:03:35.500
- My mind is being re-wired right now,

00:03:35.500 --> 00:03:37.477
like the things I knew about raw fish

00:03:37.477 --> 00:03:39.477
and the taste that you could experience.

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:42.290
- Stupid good.

00:03:42.290 --> 00:03:44.140
- I wish I didn't know about this.

00:03:44.140 --> 00:03:46.550
- We flew too close to the sun on the wing

00:03:48.080 --> 00:03:48.987
of a tuna.

00:03:48.987 --> 00:03:52.800
- [Erika] We use every bit
of the ahi in this shop.

00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:54.640
- [Josh] Hawaiians are
taught to use your resources

00:03:54.640 --> 00:03:55.540
and store them well.

00:03:55.540 --> 00:03:58.570
We catch only sizeable fish,
one hook, one line at a time.

00:03:58.570 --> 00:03:59.887
I'm not thinking about a paycheck for me.

00:03:59.887 --> 00:04:01.290
I'm thinking about
putting food on the table

00:04:01.290 --> 00:04:02.530
for many generations.

00:04:02.530 --> 00:04:03.690
- [Erika] So bones and collars

00:04:03.690 --> 00:04:05.552
that's usually trash parts of the fish...

00:04:05.552 --> 00:04:06.385
- Yeah.
- You can say.

00:04:06.385 --> 00:04:07.810
It's not trash to us that's gold.

00:04:07.810 --> 00:04:09.810
Everyone's favorite is the bag of bones.

00:04:09.810 --> 00:04:11.590
It's when we get a paper lunch bag

00:04:11.590 --> 00:04:12.427
and we just fill it to the top.

00:04:12.427 --> 00:04:13.992
- Do you just munch down on that?

00:04:13.992 --> 00:04:14.825
- Yeah totally.

00:04:14.825 --> 00:04:16.000
- Is the whole thing edible at that point?

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:16.833
- You would've sucked the
meat off of those bones.

00:04:16.833 --> 00:04:18.876
Yeah, it's exactly like a chicken wing.

00:04:18.876 --> 00:04:19.709
- [Andrew] Okay.

00:04:19.709 --> 00:04:20.542
- That bone meat.

00:04:20.542 --> 00:04:21.444
- Bone meat.

00:04:21.444 --> 00:04:22.277
- That's what I'm about.

00:04:22.277 --> 00:04:24.250
- I had no idea this was gonna happen.

00:04:24.250 --> 00:04:25.830
Imagine the size of this thing.

00:04:25.830 --> 00:04:27.460
You know that's like that on you.

00:04:27.460 --> 00:04:28.293
- Oh.

00:04:28.293 --> 00:04:29.250
- How should we attack this?

00:04:29.250 --> 00:04:31.260
- People just like to
stick their finger in it.

00:04:31.260 --> 00:04:32.630
- Oh geez.

00:04:32.630 --> 00:04:34.500
- Dip it in and you eat it.

00:04:34.500 --> 00:04:36.410
- Okay, alright I can't wait.

00:04:36.410 --> 00:04:38.010
- Oh yeah...

00:04:38.010 --> 00:04:39.545
look at that.

00:04:39.545 --> 00:04:42.560
I just ripped meat off
of a fish's neck head.

00:04:42.560 --> 00:04:46.280
Oh dude, it's so fatty and soft inside.

00:04:46.280 --> 00:04:48.560
- This is where the flavor lives.

00:04:48.560 --> 00:04:49.393
- Yeah.

00:04:49.393 --> 00:04:50.226
- Right off the bone.

00:04:50.226 --> 00:04:51.400
- Now I feel like a bear.

00:04:51.400 --> 00:04:53.699
- Can you imagine if bears
knew how to deep fry fish?

00:04:53.699 --> 00:04:54.620
(laughing)

00:04:54.620 --> 00:04:57.250
Last thing I want to talk
about is the aloha spirit.

00:04:57.250 --> 00:04:59.780
It's about the family,
it's about the neighbors

00:04:59.780 --> 00:05:01.630
- Can I eat the outside of this?

00:05:01.630 --> 00:05:03.090
- I'm trying to have
sentimental moment with you bro.

00:05:03.090 --> 00:05:05.413
- Sorry, I gotta find out if I can eat it.

00:05:05.413 --> 00:05:07.880
(chewing)

00:05:07.880 --> 00:05:09.410
- [Steven] We got Leonard's Bakery.

00:05:09.410 --> 00:05:12.330
My roommate is from Hawaii and
he said we should eat here.

00:05:12.330 --> 00:05:13.640
Oh man.

00:05:13.640 --> 00:05:15.056
Boom.

00:05:15.056 --> 00:05:17.612
(soft rock music)

00:05:17.612 --> 00:05:18.445
- Oh.

00:05:18.445 --> 00:05:19.278
- Oh no.

00:05:19.278 --> 00:05:20.940
I feel like eating a thick cloud.

00:05:20.940 --> 00:05:21.773
- It's delicious.

00:05:21.773 --> 00:05:22.606
- I need to live here.

00:05:22.606 --> 00:05:23.439
- [Andrew &amp; Steve] Fish fact.

00:05:23.439 --> 00:05:25.680
- [Andrew] The mucus-producing hagfish

00:05:25.680 --> 00:05:28.390
can fill a two gallon bucket with mucus

00:05:28.390 --> 00:05:30.750
in minutes when disturbed.

00:05:30.750 --> 00:05:31.840
- What?

00:05:31.840 --> 00:05:32.673
- Yeah.

00:05:32.673 --> 00:05:34.130
- How big is this fish?

00:05:34.130 --> 00:05:36.270
- We'll try to find a
picture and put it here.

00:05:36.270 --> 00:05:38.910
- Can you imagine
producing gallons of liquid

00:05:38.910 --> 00:05:39.810
from your body?

00:05:39.810 --> 00:05:40.643
- Okay.

00:05:40.643 --> 00:05:41.476
- Just when your annoyed.

00:05:41.476 --> 00:05:43.040
I guess that's kinda like what crying is,

00:05:43.040 --> 00:05:44.750
but if you cry gallons.

00:05:44.750 --> 00:05:46.780
- And it was thick in viscous.

00:05:46.780 --> 00:05:48.930
- Yes, great usage of viscous.

00:05:48.930 --> 00:05:50.870
- Now we're going to a
place called MW Restaurant.

00:05:50.870 --> 00:05:52.750
It is a husband-wife duo.

00:05:52.750 --> 00:05:55.160
- I wonder if they consider each other

00:05:55.160 --> 00:05:56.397
a catch.

00:05:56.397 --> 00:05:58.510
(serene music)

00:05:58.510 --> 00:05:59.957
- I'm Michelle Karr-Ueoka,

00:05:59.957 --> 00:06:02.847
pastry chef, owner of MW Restaurant.

00:06:02.847 --> 00:06:05.360
- My name is Wade Ueoka, chef
and co-owner of MW Restaurant.

00:06:05.360 --> 00:06:08.420
- I love the fork MW, it's awesome.

00:06:08.420 --> 00:06:10.300
- [Wade] Her cousin
actually designed it for us.

00:06:10.300 --> 00:06:12.741
- That was one of the very
few things we agreed on.

00:06:12.741 --> 00:06:13.574
(laughing)

00:06:13.574 --> 00:06:15.200
- What kind of restaurant
is MW Restaurant?

00:06:15.200 --> 00:06:16.470
- [Wade] We do Hawaii Regional Cuisine.

00:06:16.470 --> 00:06:18.570
Hawaii is made up of so
many ethnic backgrounds

00:06:18.570 --> 00:06:20.670
so we try to put that
all together on a plate.

00:06:20.670 --> 00:06:22.500
- Do you mainly focus on the dessert

00:06:22.500 --> 00:06:24.441
or do you cover other
areas in the restaurant?

00:06:24.441 --> 00:06:25.504
- No no, only dessert

00:06:25.504 --> 00:06:27.406
and I think that makes
it a happy marriage too.

00:06:27.406 --> 00:06:29.830
The front is his side,
the back is my side.

00:06:29.830 --> 00:06:31.270
- [Wade] Today we're making the dish

00:06:31.270 --> 00:06:33.590
for this beautiful naga
that just came in today.

00:06:33.590 --> 00:06:35.030
It's a long tail red snapper.

00:06:35.030 --> 00:06:36.657
We take the filet, season with salt,

00:06:36.657 --> 00:06:38.370
and we take blocks of kiri mochi,

00:06:38.370 --> 00:06:40.460
and then we grate it, looks
like shredded coconut.

00:06:40.460 --> 00:06:43.070
Coat the fish and then
sear it a medium to hot pan

00:06:43.070 --> 00:06:44.040
with about 3-4 minutes on each side.

00:06:44.040 --> 00:06:46.440
We place the fish right
on top several minutes

00:06:46.440 --> 00:06:48.960
and then three small piles
of bonchon vegetables,

00:06:48.960 --> 00:06:51.480
kimchi, bok choy, and
then some pickled namasu.

00:06:51.480 --> 00:06:53.710
My mom used to make deep friend mochi,

00:06:53.710 --> 00:06:55.458
growing up is one of my favorite things.

00:06:55.458 --> 00:06:56.910
That's kind of the
inspiration of this dish.

00:06:56.910 --> 00:06:58.070
- [Steven] Do you serve the dish now

00:06:58.070 --> 00:06:59.700
when you mom comes to the restaurant?

00:06:59.700 --> 00:07:00.533
- [Wade] Yes.

00:07:00.533 --> 00:07:01.405
(laughing)

00:07:01.405 --> 00:07:02.238
What does she think about that?

00:07:02.238 --> 00:07:03.447
- [Wade] She loves it.

00:07:03.447 --> 00:07:04.280
- [Andrew] This is delightful.

00:07:04.280 --> 00:07:06.580
Do you ever thing you'd
mochi as a crust for a fish?

00:07:06.580 --> 00:07:08.410
- No, but...

00:07:08.410 --> 00:07:09.760
now I'm thinking

00:07:09.760 --> 00:07:11.860
what else can we crust with mochi?

00:07:11.860 --> 00:07:12.820
- Probably anything.

00:07:12.820 --> 00:07:14.570
- This is a unnamed cocktail by the way.

00:07:14.570 --> 00:07:15.403
- Yeah.

00:07:15.403 --> 00:07:17.510
- There's still development
it's uzu, ginger.

00:07:17.510 --> 00:07:19.683
- Some kind of Japanese whiskey highball

00:07:19.683 --> 00:07:21.300
- And a local honey.

00:07:21.300 --> 00:07:24.150
- Local honey, that's what
they called me in high school.

00:07:25.290 --> 00:07:26.290
- Cheers.
- Cheers.

00:07:27.960 --> 00:07:29.610
- Okay that's delicious.

00:07:29.610 --> 00:07:31.210
In the world of crunchy stuff

00:07:31.210 --> 00:07:32.770
there are all types of
different kinds of crunch.

00:07:32.770 --> 00:07:35.490
You've got your potato chip
crunch, your carrot crunch.

00:07:35.490 --> 00:07:39.650
I would most relate this crunch
to the crunch of popcorn.

00:07:39.650 --> 00:07:42.150
Initially a crunch and
then super soft all the way

00:07:42.150 --> 00:07:43.130
through on the inside.

00:07:43.130 --> 00:07:44.313
I'm gonna dip this in the sauce.

00:07:44.313 --> 00:07:47.146
(soft rock music)

00:07:48.290 --> 00:07:50.440
- Slightly sweet, slightly salty.

00:07:50.440 --> 00:07:52.820
This is just like very slight.

00:07:52.820 --> 00:07:54.160
- Yeah, this is delightful.

00:07:54.160 --> 00:07:57.490
The snapper flesh itself
is like pats of butter

00:07:57.490 --> 00:07:58.743
with flakes apart.

00:08:00.150 --> 00:08:01.960
Yeah, fish butter.

00:08:01.960 --> 00:08:03.920
- I think fish is the perfect meat.

00:08:03.920 --> 00:08:04.753
- Yes.

00:08:04.753 --> 00:08:05.790
It's so tender and light.

00:08:05.790 --> 00:08:07.770
- Also did you see the raw snapper?

00:08:07.770 --> 00:08:08.603
- Yes.

00:08:08.603 --> 00:08:09.730
- [Andrew] It's a beautiful creature.

00:08:09.730 --> 00:08:11.760
People think horses are beautiful

00:08:11.760 --> 00:08:13.760
that is a red ocean dragon.

00:08:13.760 --> 00:08:14.970
- I'm not gonna to forget this moment.

00:08:14.970 --> 00:08:16.980
I'm gonna be telling my
kids and my grandkids

00:08:16.980 --> 00:08:18.370
about this mochi crusted fish.

00:08:18.370 --> 00:08:19.430
I'm going home tomorrow

00:08:19.430 --> 00:08:20.590
and I'm gonna try to make this myself.

00:08:20.590 --> 00:08:21.860
Pop this whole thing in your mouth.

00:08:21.860 --> 00:08:22.700
You want some sauce, here.

00:08:22.700 --> 00:08:24.175
Take a little bit of sauce.

00:08:24.175 --> 00:08:25.416
- Drizzle, yeah.

00:08:25.416 --> 00:08:27.793
His minds gonna be blown.

00:08:30.360 --> 00:08:31.419
Yeah.

00:08:31.419 --> 00:08:32.252
Time for dessert.

00:08:32.252 --> 00:08:34.830
- For dessert you'll be having
tropical creamsicle brulee.

00:08:34.830 --> 00:08:36.350
Coconut tapioca on the bottom,

00:08:36.350 --> 00:08:38.880
which is a play of of the
Filipino dish halo-halo.

00:08:38.880 --> 00:08:41.010
And then we top it off
with tropical fruits,

00:08:41.010 --> 00:08:43.640
lilikoi custard, my
version of a gummy bear.

00:08:43.640 --> 00:08:45.810
I loved gummy bears growing up.

00:08:45.810 --> 00:08:47.070
I take the lilikoi

00:08:47.070 --> 00:08:49.560
and there's a french
dessert called petit four,

00:08:49.560 --> 00:08:52.280
it's a little bit softer
not as chewy and gummy

00:08:52.280 --> 00:08:53.500
so I combined the two together

00:08:53.500 --> 00:08:55.220
to make the texture of a gummy bear.

00:08:55.220 --> 00:08:57.410
Lilikoi sorbet, lilikoi custard,

00:08:57.410 --> 00:08:59.920
and we burn it like a thin sugar cookie.

00:08:59.920 --> 00:09:00.753
It all comes together.

00:09:00.753 --> 00:09:03.250
- [Andrew] Creme brulee
might be my favorite dessert.

00:09:03.250 --> 00:09:05.149
It's neck and neck with tiramisu

00:09:05.149 --> 00:09:08.033
and carrot cake, and gelato.

00:09:08.033 --> 00:09:08.866
(laughing)

00:09:08.866 --> 00:09:09.945
I'm ready to break the crust.

00:09:09.945 --> 00:09:12.362
(jazz music)

00:09:14.970 --> 00:09:16.220
- It's over.

00:09:16.220 --> 00:09:17.130
- Yeah.

00:09:17.130 --> 00:09:18.160
- One fish, two fish.

00:09:18.160 --> 00:09:19.453
- Screw fish eat dessert.

00:09:19.453 --> 00:09:20.354
(laughing)

00:09:20.354 --> 00:09:22.130
Oh my god.

00:09:22.130 --> 00:09:25.070
The sorbet inside is so good.

00:09:25.070 --> 00:09:27.220
I like how you double every texture.

00:09:27.220 --> 00:09:28.550
You have something chewy

00:09:28.550 --> 00:09:30.030
and something slightly different chewy.

00:09:30.030 --> 00:09:32.430
The gummy bear cube and the tapioca balls.

00:09:32.430 --> 00:09:33.780
It's like a gummy bear,

00:09:33.780 --> 00:09:37.490
but it doesn't have the
part of a gummy bear

00:09:37.490 --> 00:09:39.330
that sticks your teeth together.

00:09:39.330 --> 00:09:40.980
- I used to be-head gummy bears as a kid.

00:09:40.980 --> 00:09:42.110
- Oh yeah, you gotta
be-head the gummy bear.

00:09:42.110 --> 00:09:44.853
- And then you swap the heads
with the different colors.

00:09:44.853 --> 00:09:45.803
- I didn't do that.

00:09:52.260 --> 00:09:54.640
- [Andrew] Chef Michelle's
mother hand bakes

00:09:54.640 --> 00:09:56.297
her grandmother's recipe.

00:09:56.297 --> 00:09:57.652
Cookie cheers.

00:09:57.652 --> 00:09:58.520
- That's what I'm talking about.

00:09:58.520 --> 00:09:59.353
Cheers.

00:10:01.490 --> 00:10:02.780
- That's really good.

00:10:02.780 --> 00:10:03.870
Got a fish fact for me?

00:10:03.870 --> 00:10:04.907
- Fish fact

00:10:04.907 --> 00:10:05.740
(symbol clash)

00:10:05.740 --> 00:10:06.683
Actually its more like fish trivia.

00:10:06.683 --> 00:10:08.114
Although, many species have
not been discovered yet

00:10:08.114 --> 00:10:11.803
right now how many fish
species do you think there are?

00:10:12.930 --> 00:10:13.763
- Fifty thousand.

00:10:13.763 --> 00:10:15.480
- Your in the right ball park.

00:10:15.480 --> 00:10:17.400
- The answer is 27,300.

00:10:17.400 --> 00:10:18.930
- That's a lot of fish species.

00:10:18.930 --> 00:10:20.230
I mean the ocean is giant.

00:10:20.230 --> 00:10:21.600
It's like the rest of the world

00:10:21.600 --> 00:10:23.121
is living on a one story building

00:10:23.121 --> 00:10:26.650
and the ocean is a
thousand story building.

00:10:26.650 --> 00:10:27.630
Fish on every floor.

00:10:27.630 --> 00:10:30.060
Now we're on our way to Chef Mavro

00:10:30.060 --> 00:10:31.250
- What are we eating there?

00:10:31.250 --> 00:10:32.431
- Fish.

00:10:32.431 --> 00:10:35.181
(soothing music)

00:10:47.640 --> 00:10:49.160
- [Andrew] What type of cuisine do you

00:10:49.160 --> 00:10:50.110
here at Chef Mavro?

00:11:09.120 --> 00:11:10.523
- Really, why?

00:11:16.134 --> 00:11:20.134
- Tell us about the onaga
that you used in this.

00:11:43.320 --> 00:11:46.020
- [Andrew] Is the tea leaf
a local ingredient as well?

00:11:52.360 --> 00:11:53.601
- [Andrew] That's the leaf.

00:11:53.601 --> 00:11:54.934
- [Andrew] Okay.

00:12:05.063 --> 00:12:06.230
- [Andrew] Oh.

00:12:11.780 --> 00:12:13.350
- [Andrew] Is there a recommended beverage

00:12:13.350 --> 00:12:15.050
that we should have along with it?

00:12:34.370 --> 00:12:35.511
- [Steven] Thank you.

00:12:35.511 --> 00:12:36.344
Cheers.

00:12:38.491 --> 00:12:40.034
- Divine.
- Smooth.

00:12:40.034 --> 00:12:41.013
- Ready to have this fish?

00:12:43.568 --> 00:12:47.490
- [Andrew] Do you always serve the fish?

00:12:47.490 --> 00:12:48.323
- When I can.

00:12:48.323 --> 00:12:50.317
Sometimes we get too
busy, I cannot escape.

00:12:50.317 --> 00:12:51.850
- So this guy...

00:12:54.080 --> 00:12:55.527
- Alright.

00:12:55.527 --> 00:12:56.774
- You cannot eat that with all this salt.

00:12:56.774 --> 00:12:58.367
Even you want I'm not
going to give to you.

00:12:59.634 --> 00:13:00.695
- Enjoy it.

00:13:00.695 --> 00:13:01.840
- Thank you.
- Thank you so much.

00:13:01.840 --> 00:13:04.720
I did not expect any of this.

00:13:04.720 --> 00:13:08.395
You literally create a
shell to cook the fish in.

00:13:08.395 --> 00:13:10.900
(smelling)

00:13:10.900 --> 00:13:13.460
- I honestly don't know
what's happening here

00:13:13.460 --> 00:13:14.440
with my nose.

00:13:14.440 --> 00:13:16.980
Fish is in there, but there's
so much more happening.

00:13:16.980 --> 00:13:17.813
Cheers.

00:13:20.200 --> 00:13:21.460
- What?

00:13:21.460 --> 00:13:22.293
- What.

00:13:22.293 --> 00:13:24.450
- More unexpected things
that are happening right now.

00:13:24.450 --> 00:13:25.850
I need to take another bite.

00:13:28.081 --> 00:13:31.710
Okay, I have to say it's
extremely delicious.

00:13:31.710 --> 00:13:34.450
- I've never had a more
perfectly cooked fish.

00:13:34.450 --> 00:13:36.550
- I don't understand what is happening.

00:13:36.550 --> 00:13:38.400
My expectation going in, I was like okay,

00:13:38.400 --> 00:13:41.057
I know something tastes like
a tomato, with leaf and fish.

00:13:41.057 --> 00:13:43.540
For my mouth it is not at all that.

00:13:43.540 --> 00:13:44.640
- Here's my impression of it

00:13:44.640 --> 00:13:45.930
and this is gonna sound ridiculous

00:13:45.930 --> 00:13:48.150
because I know that we just interviewed

00:13:48.150 --> 00:13:49.500
a french man who moved to Hawaii,

00:13:49.500 --> 00:13:51.380
but when I eat this fish

00:13:51.380 --> 00:13:52.712
the first thing I think of is

00:13:52.712 --> 00:13:54.651
a french man who moved to Hawaii

00:13:54.651 --> 00:13:55.484
(laughing)

00:13:55.484 --> 00:13:56.890
and made the most delicious fish he could.

00:13:56.890 --> 00:13:58.010
That's what it tastes like.

00:13:58.010 --> 00:13:58.843
I don't know.

00:13:58.843 --> 00:14:01.790
The seaweed inside the
sauce is blowing my mind.

00:14:01.790 --> 00:14:04.140
- It all feels like one delicious,

00:14:04.140 --> 00:14:06.840
mind melting, in heart warming texture.

00:14:06.840 --> 00:14:08.150
- It kind of just tastes like

00:14:08.150 --> 00:14:10.010
that's how this fish always tasted.

00:14:10.010 --> 00:14:11.420
- Yeah, I'm trying to think of

00:14:11.420 --> 00:14:13.690
what could be just
perfectly come together?

00:14:13.690 --> 00:14:17.461
- I would also never guess
spinach was gonna end up in here.

00:14:17.461 --> 00:14:18.513
- No.

00:14:18.513 --> 00:14:19.346
- Oh.

00:14:19.346 --> 00:14:20.480
- That looks like a delicious biscuit

00:14:20.480 --> 00:14:22.020
that's covered in sugar right now.

00:14:22.020 --> 00:14:23.653
- I want to eat it,
but I know chef told us

00:14:23.653 --> 00:14:25.683
not to let our taste buds near that.

00:14:29.102 --> 00:14:30.423
- Just makes sense.

00:14:31.890 --> 00:14:33.940
- [Andrew] The movie with Robert De Niro?

00:14:38.900 --> 00:14:40.700
- I know this is the
end of the fish video,

00:14:40.700 --> 00:14:42.733
but we're here where the fish get made.

00:14:42.733 --> 00:14:43.566
(laughing)

00:14:43.566 --> 00:14:45.060
- [Steven] One of my favorite episodes

00:14:45.060 --> 00:14:46.150
that we've never done.

00:14:46.150 --> 00:14:47.440
- Yeah, me too.

00:14:47.440 --> 00:14:48.500
- Aye oh!

00:14:48.500 --> 00:14:49.460
Who's your worth it winner?

00:14:49.460 --> 00:14:50.430
- [Andrew] MW Restaurant,

00:14:50.430 --> 00:14:51.820
they just do such a good job there.

00:14:51.820 --> 00:14:55.000
Everything is delicious,
but my worth it winner

00:14:55.000 --> 00:14:56.040
Chef Mavro.

00:14:56.040 --> 00:14:56.873
- [Steven] No way.

00:14:56.873 --> 00:14:58.930
- Chef Mavro's dish is the story of a man

00:14:58.930 --> 00:15:01.400
who moved to Hawaii, fell
in love with the island,

00:15:01.400 --> 00:15:03.714
and then created a masterpiece on a plate.

00:15:03.714 --> 00:15:04.547
- Wow.

00:15:04.547 --> 00:15:05.493
- I think you just want to move here.

00:15:05.493 --> 00:15:06.930
- I just want to be Chef Mavro.

00:15:06.930 --> 00:15:08.200
What was your worth it winner?

00:15:08.200 --> 00:15:09.380
- [Steven] The poke at Assassins.

00:15:09.380 --> 00:15:10.213
- [Andrew] Whoa.

00:15:10.213 --> 00:15:11.046
- [Steven] Yeah.

00:15:11.046 --> 00:15:12.439
- They killed it.

00:15:12.439 --> 00:15:13.272
(laughing)

00:15:13.272 --> 00:15:14.391
Literally.

00:15:14.391 --> 00:15:15.396
(laughing)

00:15:15.396 --> 00:15:17.650
- Poke in general doesn't
really make sense to me

00:15:17.650 --> 00:15:18.483
other than here.

00:15:18.483 --> 00:15:20.710
If your in Ohio eating
poke the cost of freight

00:15:20.710 --> 00:15:22.267
is not sustainable.

00:15:22.267 --> 00:15:23.610
- Did you say fright?

00:15:23.610 --> 00:15:24.443
- Freight.

00:15:24.443 --> 00:15:25.276
- Oh, freight.

00:15:25.276 --> 00:15:26.400
- Freight.
- Freight.

00:15:26.400 --> 00:15:27.410
- Is it not freight?

00:15:27.410 --> 00:15:28.779
- You said fright.

00:15:28.779 --> 00:15:29.863
The cost of scaring the fish today.

00:15:29.863 --> 00:15:30.818
(laughing)

00:15:30.818 --> 00:15:32.400
Adam who's your worth it winner?

00:15:32.400 --> 00:15:33.930
- The creme brulee?

00:15:33.930 --> 00:15:35.280
- Michelle's creme brulee.

00:15:35.280 --> 00:15:36.800
- I actually haven't touched the ocean yet

00:15:36.800 --> 00:15:37.940
since I've been here.

00:15:37.940 --> 00:15:39.163
- I'm going first.

00:15:40.840 --> 00:15:41.673
- Is it warm?

00:15:43.884 --> 00:15:44.717
(waves crashing)

00:15:44.717 --> 00:15:46.300
Oh it's nice.

00:15:46.300 --> 00:15:47.330
- See you on the next episode.

00:15:47.330 --> 00:15:48.163
What are we doing next?

00:15:48.163 --> 00:15:49.120
- Bye.
- Bye.

00:15:49.120 --> 00:15:50.010
Bye guys.

00:15:50.010 --> 00:15:50.843
- Bye.

00:15:50.843 --> 00:15:51.676
- [Steven] I want to be a fisherman.

00:15:51.676 --> 00:15:52.509
You ever fish?

00:15:52.509 --> 00:15:53.342
- [Andrew] Couple times as a kid.

00:15:53.342 --> 00:15:54.470
A lot more crabbing now.

00:15:54.470 --> 00:15:55.720
I don't know if that
counts as fish or not.

00:15:55.720 --> 00:15:56.580
- [Steven] Are crab fish?

00:15:56.580 --> 00:15:57.450
- [Andrew] No, they're not.

00:15:57.450 --> 00:16:00.223
You catch a fish, you capture a crab.

00:16:01.250 --> 00:16:02.383
- [Steven] Oh, yes!

